{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3ad05f52\" lines=\"128-142\">\n<description>\nPaint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.\n- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.\n\nRule\n- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.\n- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.\n- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.\n\nKey insight\n- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b59e163\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nCopy each marked sprite to every standalone marker of the same color and erase the original sources.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each source is an 8-connected component containing a monochrome sprite and one marker pixel of another color.\n- Other isolated marker pixels may be destinations; marker colors with no source sprite are distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the grid to background.\n- For every source marker color, place the sprite at each other marker of that color using the source marker as the anchor.\n- Keep each destination marker pixel in place; do not keep source sprites, source markers, or distractor markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- A colored marker is not part of the sprite geometry; it is the anchor that says where same-colored copies of that sprite should be stamped.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2a28add5\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\n  in that row are counters.\nRule\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\nKey insight\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342dd610\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nMove every foreground pixel by the fixed offset indicated by its color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a unique dominant background color.\n- The foreground is a sparse set of colored pixels.\n- Each foreground color is one of the trained motion colors: orange, red,\n  maroon, or blue.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n- Translate orange pixels up 2 cells, red pixels left 2 cells, maroon\n  pixels down 2 cells, and blue pixels right 1 cell.\n- Preserve each moved pixel's original color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe foreground color is the motion instruction; position only determines\nwhere that color-specific displacement lands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b9c3697\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nInsert red components into interior sockets of large structures, leaving a black trail.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common. Large non-bg, non-red structures with interior cavities.\n- Red components outside structures.\n\nRule:\n- Interior sockets: bg cells inside a structure's bbox, adjacent to structure pixels.\n- For each structure, find the direction where rays from sockets hit a red component.\n- Slide that component into the socket; paint traversed cells black.\n- Unmatched reds vanish.\n\nKey insight: Docking direction is inferred by ray-casting from interior sockets to the nearest red component.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d6c6e23\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nMove each vertical stream of pixels into a bottom-aligned centered pile.\n\nInput structure\n- A mostly background grid contains one or more vertical source columns of\n  foreground pixels.\n- Each source column has n^2 foreground pixels, and the top-to-bottom color\n  sequence splits into monochrome groups of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nRule\n- Clear the canvas to background.\n- For each source column, read the foreground colors from top to bottom and\n  rebuild them at the bottom below the same column as odd-width centered\n  rows of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered vertical pixels are falling grains; a square number of\n  grains decomposes into the odd row widths of a centered pyramid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"36a08778\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nSimulate water falling from top-edge sources, pooling on shelves, and cascading off edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; water = rarest non-bg; shelf = other non-bg.\n- Sources: water clusters touching top row. Shelves: horizontal segments.\n\nRule:\n- Water falls down from sources, painting bg cells as water.\n- On hitting a shelf, it spreads across the shelf span plus one cell past each end.\n- From those overflow positions, water resumes falling independently.\n\nKey insight: Each shelf splits a stream into two at its edges, creating a branching waterfall tree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b745798f\" lines=\"18-33\">\n<description>\nExpand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a\n  2x2 box.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to the background.\n- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner\n  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.\n\nKey insight\n- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,\n  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7256dcd\" lines=\"18-32\">\n<description>\nTransfer each colored marker to its adjacent neutral mask component, then erase the markers.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color.\n- The neutral mask color is the unique most frequent foreground color.\n- Other foreground colors are marker pixels that may touch neutral 4-connected mask components.\nRule\n- Remove every marker pixel by recoloring it to the background.\n- For each 4-connected neutral mask component, find marker colors touching it by edge adjacency.\n- If a component touches one marker color, recolor the whole neutral component to that marker color.\n- If a component touches no marker color, leave it neutral.\nKey insight\n- Marker pixels label neighboring neutral regions but are not part of the final colored objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c416de3\" lines=\"99-118\">\n<description>\nReplicate a pre-drawn corner pattern to every other marker-bearing corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most frequent color. Hollow 0-frames (possibly partial L-shapes); each\n  frame-corner cell is either 0 or a painted (non-bg, non-0) color.\n- >= 1 'source' corner: the pattern is already drawn (one color, both outward\n  cells and an inward-diagonal trail).\n- Other corners: a single colored pixel on the inward diagonal at the pattern's\n  deepest (k,k) offset; the pixel's color is that corner's target color.\n\nRule:\n- Extract the canonical (TL-oriented) pattern from any source.\n- For each non-source corner whose marker cell is colored, paint the same\n  pattern rotated to that corner's orientation, using the marker's color.\n\nKey insight: the pattern is symmetric about the corner's inward-diagonal axis\n(canonical dr == dc), so it reflects cleanly across all four corner orientations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
